Q: How do I solve `malloc(): corrupted top size`? A: <p>For starters there are numerous memory leaks at least due to the function <strong>cleanLine</strong></p>
<pre><code>char* cleanLine(char* d, FILE* fp) {
    char* c = strdup(d);
    //....
    return c;
}
</code></pre>
<p>where there is used function <strong>strdup</strong> that dynamically allocates memory that is not freed in the program. Moreover the address of the allocated memory can be lost.</p>
<p>And the address of the initial value of the pointer <strong>instruction</strong> that also points to dynamically allocated memory also is lost due to this statement</p>
<pre><code>instruction = cleanLine(instruction, fp);
</code></pre>
<p>As a result such calls as that</p>
<pre><code>fgets(instruction, 100, fp) 
</code></pre>
<p>invoke undefined behavior.</p>
<p>Looking at the code I do not see a reason why you are allocating memory dynamically for a character array where you are reading records from the file. It would be much sumpler and safer to declare a character array</p>
<pre><code>char instruction[100];
</code></pre>
<p>Also separating calls of <strong>fgets</strong> between <strong>main</strong> and  the function <strong>cleanLine</strong> is a bad design. It would be much better to write a function that returns to <strong>main</strong> a next command or <strong>NULL</strong> from the file. At present within the while loop</p>
<pre><code> while (fgets(instruction, 100, fp) != NULL) {
     instruction = cleanLine(instruction, fp);
</code></pre>
<p>the pointer <strong>instruction</strong> can be a <strong>NULL</strong> pointer returned by the function <strong>cleanLine</strong>.</p>
<p>And the function <strong>instructionType</strong></p>
<pre><code>int instructionType(char* c) {
    if (*c == '(') return TYPE_L;
    else if (*c == '@') return TYPE_A;
    else return TYPE_C;
}
</code></pre>
<p>can also invoke undefined behavior if the value of the first character of the passed string is not one of the listed values in the if-else statements. Instead of these macros</p>
<pre><code>#define TYPE_A 0
#define TYPE_C 1
#define TYPE_L 2
</code></pre>
<p>It is better to declare an enumeration adding one more element that signals a bad command.</p>
<p>So my advice is redesign your program entirely.</p>
<p>for example you could define a function named like getNextInstruction the bfollowing way</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;ctype.h&gt;

size_t N = 100;

char * getNextInstruction( char *buffer, FILE *fp )
{
    char comment = '/';
    bool found = false;
    char *first = NULL;

    while ( !found &amp;&amp; fgets( buffer, N, fp ) != NULL )
    {
        size_t n = strcspn( buffer, &quot;\n&quot; );
        buffer[ n ] = '\0'; 

        first = buffer;

        while ( isblank( *first ) ) ++first;

        if ( *first != '\0' &amp;&amp; *first != comment )
        {
            char *last = buffer + n;

            while ( isblank( last[-1] ) ) --last;

            *last = '\0';

            found = true;
        }
    }

    return found ? first : NULL;
}
</code></pre>
<p>And in <strong>main</strong> you need to declare a pointer that will accept a returned value from the function.</p>
<p>for example</p>
<pre><code>char buffer[N];

char *instruction = getNextInstruction( buffer, fp );

if ( instruction != NULL )
{
    // check the instruction and do something else if required 
}
</code></pre>
